4215 13194139534525 17592186045417 true] [17592186424224 1079 #uuid "f8def5ae-54bc-5cde-af27-af1ce38db335" 13194139534525 17592186045417 true] [17592186424225 1075 #edb/ref 17592186424199 13194139534525 17592186045417 true] [17592186424225 1075 #edb/ref 17592186424205 13194139534525 17592186045417 true] [17592186424225 1075 #edb/ref 17592186424210 13194139534525 17592186045417 true] [17592186424225 1075 #edb/ref 17592186424211 13194139534525 17592186045417 true] [17592186424225 1075 #edb/ref 17592186424213 13194139534525 17592186045417 true] [17592186424225 1076 #edb/ref 17592186045493 13194139534525 17592186045417 true] [17592186424225 1077 "selection" 13194139534525 17592186045417 true] [17592186424225 1078 #edb/ref 17592186424211 13194139534525 17592186045417 true] [17592186424225 1079 #uuid "fadbbb52-eaa9-5960-9164-0e7ea16514af" 13194139534525 17592186045417 true] [17592186424226 1068 #edb/ref 17592186424225 13194139534525 17592186045417 true] [17592186424226 1069 "q-123172" 13194139534525 17592186045417 true] [17592186424226 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139534525 17592186045417 true] [17592186424226 1071 "Given that $F=\\sqrt{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}}\\,i-\\sqrt{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}}\\,j,$ what is $\\text{div}\\,F?$" 13194139534525 17592186045417 true] [17592186424226 1072 #uuid "162e9632-ec96-53eb-bf5e-ab2bd97ae9e0" 13194139534525 17592186045417 true] [17592186424226 1074 "If $F=P\\,i+Q\\,j$ is a vector field on ${R}^{2}$ and the partial derivatives of the components of $F$ all exist, then the divergence of $F$ is the scalar field given by\n\n$$\n\\text{div}\\,F=∇\\cdot F=\\frac{∂P}{∂x}+\\frac{∂Q}{∂y}.\n$$\n\n  \n\nTherefore, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}∇\\cdot F & =\\frac{∂P}{∂x}+\\frac{∂Q}{∂y} \\\\  & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}(\\sqrt{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}})+\\frac{∂}{∂y}(-\\sqrt{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}}) \\\\  & =\\frac{x}{\\sqrt{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}}}-\\frac{y}{\\sqrt{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}}} \\\\  & =\\frac{x-y}{\\sqrt{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139534525 17592186045417 true] [17592186424227 1068 #edb/ref 17592186424224 13194139534525 17592186045417 true] [17592186424227 1069 "q-131195" 13194139534525 17592186045417 true] [17592186424227 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045486 13194139534525 17592186045417 true] [17592186424227 1071 "![](images/39/39dfec02c787141d8a728381f4093dfe9cbf3fdbf996177ba6f24d973015dc5f.png)\n\nConsider the vector field $F$ shown above. Which of the following statements are true everywhere in the domain of $F?$\n\nI. $∇\\cdot F=0$\n\nII. $∇\\cdot F>0$\n\nIII. $∇\\cdot F<0$\n\nIV. $F$ is solenoidal" 13194139534525 17592186045417 true] [17592186424227 1072 #uuid "5777673f-cba1-5775-ae01-f18d4531f239" 13194139534525 17592186045417 true] [17592186424227 1074 "Recall that $\\text{div}\\,F=∇\\cdot F$ denotes the divergence of the vector field $F.$\n\nIntuitively, the divergence of $F$ at a point $P$ measures how much $F$ acts like a source or a sink at $P:$\n\n- If $F$ acts like a source at $P,$ then $∇\\cdot F>0$ at $P.$\n\n- If $F$ acts like a sink at $P,$ then $∇\\cdot F<0$ at $P.$\n\n- If $F$ acts like neither a source nor a sink at $P,$ then $∇\\cdot F=0$ at $P.$\n\n![](images/6c/6c747149618d4c5ef35e727af15cd001365bd9c2d5e064689baf24c6ee23cff9.png)\n\nIf $∇\\cdot F=0$ everywhere in the domain of $F,$ we say that $F$ is solenoidal.\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine each statement:\n\n- Statement II is true, whereas statements I and III are false. Notice that the lengths of the vector's $y$-components increase as we move vertically away from the origin. Therefore, the vector field $F$ acts like a source at every point.\n\n- Statement IV is false. Since $∇\\cdot F\\ne 0,$ the vector field $F$ is not solenoidal.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"II only.\"" 13194139534525 17592186045417 true] [17592186424228 1068 #edb/ref 17592186424223 13194139534525 17592186045417 true] [17592186424228 1069 "q-131196" 13194139534525 17592186045417 true] [17592186424228 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045486 13194139534525 17592186045417 true] [17592186424228 1071 "![](images/4b/4bf8de7e8719545b3d8abf5dc9fb4bc0c5f71034dba7186b7d9822d9e8be044d.png)\n\nConsider the vector field $F$ shown above. Which of the following statements are true everywhere in the domain of $F?$\n\nI. $∇\\cdot F=0$\n\nII. $∇\\cdot F>0$\n\nIII. $∇\\cdot F<0$\n\nIV. $F$ is solenoidal" 13194139534525 17592186045417 true] [17592186424228 1072 #uuid "9278ec55-4908-5c1c-8a74-640611473537" 13194139534525 17592186045417 true] [17592186424228 1074 "Recall that $\\text{div}\\,F=∇\\cdot F$ denotes the divergence of the vector field $F.$\n\nIntuitively, the divergence of $F$ at a point $P$ measures how much $F$ acts like a source or a sink at $P:$\n\n- If $F$ acts like a source at $P,$ then $∇\\cdot F>0$ at $P.$\n\n- If $F$ acts like a sink at $P,$ then $∇\\cdot F<0$ at $P.$\n\n- If $F$ acts like neither a source nor a sink at $P,$ then $∇\\cdot F=0$ at $P.$\n\n![](images/6c/6c747149618d4c5ef35e727af15cd001365bd9c2d5e064689baf24c6ee23cff9.png)\n\nIf $∇\\cdot F=0$ everywhere in the domain of $F,$ we say that $F$ is solenoidal.\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine each statement:\n\n- Statement III is true, whereas statements I and II are false. Notice that the lengths of the vector's $x$-components decrease as we move horizontally towards the origin. Therefore, the vector field $F$ acts like a sink at every point.\n\n- Statement IV is false. Since $∇\\cdot F\\ne 0,$ the vector field $F$ is not solenoidal.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"III only.\"" 13194139534525 17592186045417 true] [17592186424229 1068 #edb/ref 17592186424222 13194139534525 17592186045417 true] [17592186424229 1069 "q-105562" 13194139534525 17592186045417 true] [17592186424229 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139534525 17592186045417 true] [17592186424229 1071 "Consider the vector field $F={y}^{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2x)\\,i+x\\operatorname{ln}⁡y\\,j.$ \nEvaluate $\\text{div}\\,F$ at the point $(\\pi ,1).$" 13194139534525 17592186045417 true] [17592186424229 1072 #uuid "ca50a185-e281-53e0-900c-05cc2ac6e7a5" 13194139534525 17592186045417 true] [17592186424229 1074 "If $F=P\\,i+Q\\,j$ is a vector field on ${R}^{2}$ and the partial derivatives of the components of $F$ all exist, then the divergence of $F$ is the scalar field given by\n\n$$\n\\text{div}\\,F=∇\\cdot F=\\frac{∂P}{∂x}+\\frac{∂Q}{∂y}.\n$$\n\n  \n\nTherefore, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}∇\\cdot F & =\\frac{∂P}{∂x}+\\frac{∂Q}{∂y} \\\\  & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}({y}^{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2x))+\\frac{∂}{∂y}(x\\operatorname{ln}⁡y) \\\\  & =2{y}^{2}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2x)+\\frac{x}{y}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we evaluate $\\text{div}\\,F$ at the point $(\\pi ,1),$ and obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(∇\\cdot F)(\\pi ,1) & =2(1)^{2}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2\\pi )+\\frac{\\pi }{1} \\\\  & =2(1)(1)+\\pi  \\\\  & =2+\\pi .\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139534525 17592186045417 true] [17592186424230 1068 #edb/ref 17592186424221 13194139534525 17592186045417 true] [17592186424230 1069 "q-123196" 13194139534525 17592186045417 true] [17592186424230 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139534525 17592186045417 true] [17592186424230 1071 "Consider the vector field $F=(x+y)^{2}\\,i+(y+z)^{2}\\,j+(x+z)^{2}\\,k.$ Evaluate $\\text{div}\\,F$ at the point $(-2,1,-1).$" 13194139534525 17592186045417 true] [17592186424230 1072 #uuid "df216990-7a71-5841-8a5b-955dc6939d8d" 13194139534525 17592186045417 true] [17592186424230 1074 "If $F=P\\,i+Q\\,j+R\\,k$ is a vector field on ${R}^{3}$ and the partial derivatives of the components of $F$ all exist, then the divergence of $F$ is the scalar field given by\n\n$$\n\\text{div}\\,F=∇\\cdot F=\\frac{∂P}{∂x}+\\frac{∂Q}{∂y}+\\frac{∂R}{∂z}.\n$$\n\n  \n\nTherefore, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}∇\\cdot F & =\\frac{∂P}{∂x}+\\frac{∂Q}{∂y}+\\frac{∂R}{∂z} \\\\  & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}((x+y)^{2})+\\frac{∂}{∂y}((y+z)^{2})+\\frac{∂}{∂z}((x+z)^{2}) \\\\  & =2(x+y)+2(y+z)+2(x+z) \\\\  & =4(x+y+z).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we evaluate $\\text{div}\\,F$ at the point $(-2,1,-1),$ and obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{div}\\,F(-2,1,-1) & =4((-2)+(1)+(-1)) \\\\  & =4\\cdot (-2) \\\\  & =-8.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139534525 17592186045417 true]] :edb/tempids {"answer-0c5b1d09-1da1-519d-9990-fff8f06520f0" 17592186424196 "answer-18f86bf7-5c17-5cb6-8c9a-25e3a30c5a86" 17592186424197 "answer-193ddeed-a793-53c6-ba1e-92e68b0f73f0" 17592186424198 "answer-21305c88-eeaf-5820-af29-34723d616234" 17592186424199 "answer-2181d067-a072-5d02-8afa-0c943d7e22d4" 17592186424200 "answer-26003085-8363-5ee2-8473-69f3ba602654" 17592186424201 "answer-4452f92a-63c8-5376-8e98-947775e29e69" 17592186424202 "answer-4849233c-e677-553e-b525-8ce1ed84e89f" 17592186424203 "answer-4bd6eba8-c859-5a8d-b522-f2c3b0ee5a65" 17592186424204 "answer-507360f0-f40d-51b4-8d0a-a73fd33bb756" 17592186424205 "answer-51328c89-8cdd-5ec3-96c7-541a1dd6496d" 17592186424206 "answer-53e20d90-4e62-516b-ae3d-39f75e6f0b26" 17592186424207 "answer-55a8bdef-6d67-5f62-8f8a-3f474c61f4a6" 17592186424208 "answer-6e36ebd6-f283-5b52-b5a9-f667a2f4b389" 17592186424209 "answer-754db6a7-5db4-5e7b-9b14-ca330f6671e0" 17592186424210 "answer-88c4b453-3bda-58b8-8fbb-6a0d6916e90a" 17592186424211 "answer-8aff9788-bb7e-5bb2-9074-85e6460dae3e" 17592186424212 "answer-952b9fe9-4b9b-5eac-bcfa-e800b3b1094b" 17592186424213 "answer-af6e0ce3-9249-535e-9fb2-893259204ce1" 17592186424214 "answer-b1ea2773-828b-51a5-b56b-8c1ce5f3d0df" 17592186424215 "answer-b75001d5-d554-5e4b-9dfe-3ece61afe366" 17592186424216 "answer-c14c9ebe-eb84-5d9c-8dd2-5d0a1d71c120" 17592186424217 "answer-cd47d87e-fdba-55d2-b443-02e11d1f6d93" 17592186424218 "answer-cd60ac0d-9b78-5c69-bd7b-06d4c14bdb66" 17592186424219 "answer-f47d0d8f-c2bd-587b-8969-e8a2b85184c9" 17592186424220 "answer-field-3452daaf-b0f7-51b4-a94c-d00ee7b3816f" 17592186424221 "answer-field-6e9bf416-fabf-5d02-bf76-781ab322815f" 17592186424222 "answer-field-7fd542c0-4d3b-584d-9d7b-3c0d0b5750ae" 17592186424223 "answer-field-f8def5ae-54bc-5cde-af27-af1ce38db335" 17592186424224 "answer-field-fadbbb52-eaa9-5960-916